        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Educational production reveals the importance of access",
        "content": "Educational media provide a useful example of the relationship between production and delivery. UNESCO's archived technology profile for Iran identifies television, online, and radio channels used for distance education during the COVID-19 response. This demonstrates that educational content can move through several media rather than belong to a single format. The profile is historical evidence, not confirmation that each arrangement remains unchanged. [s4]\n\nAvailability also differs from accessibility. The published abstract of a qualitative study by Negin Hosseini Goodrich and Abouzar Samiei argues that the move to online schooling reproduced barriers experienced by Iranian students with disabilities. Its findings concern pandemic education and should not be generalized into a measurement of every contemporary platform. Nevertheless, the distinction is central to understanding content production: completing a lesson or uploading a file does not by itself demonstrate that its intended audience can use it. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
