    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Written content in Iran connects authorship and translation with a developed tradition of Persian editing. Preparing that work for digital readers also requires attention to script direction, mixed-language text, and the distinction between editorial responsibility and publication rights.",
    "tags": ["Persian writing", "Editing", "Translation", "Publishing history", "Digital typography"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:35:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:35:12Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Written Content in Iran: Editing and Persian Text | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Persian written-content production through editing history, translation, digital text layout, and the limits of historical copyright evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "editing-as-a-distinct-craft",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Writing reaches readers through editing",
        "content": "Iranian written-content production has a history in which editing became an identifiable professional function. The Persian term virayesh refers to editing, while virastar denotes an editor. Karim Emami's account in Encyclopaedia Iranica describes the gradual acceptance of these terms alongside the development of publishing institutions. They identify work performed on a text after, or alongside, its initial composition. [s1]\n\nTranslation was an important setting for this development. Editors checked Persian translations against their originals, looking for omissions and errors while also addressing style and spelling consistency. Scholarly publishing introduced another concern: establishing a dependable text from manuscripts and recording significant variants. These practices distinguish editing a translation, preparing a critical edition, and correcting a proof; each involves a different relationship to the underlying work. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "translation-and-publishing-infrastructure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Translation programmes helped build publishing capacity",
        "content": "The Franklin Book Program offers a concrete example of the institutions behind written production. An American nonprofit working internationally, it sought to strengthen publishing in local languages. Its Iranian programme began in 1954. Datus C. Smith Jr.'s historical account describes an arrangement in which local staff and publishers played central roles, with support extending to translation rights and the work of translation. [s2]\n\nThis history matters because a translated book is more than a transferred text. Decisions about selection, adaptation, editorial preparation, and publication shape the resulting work. Franklin's involvement in a Persian encyclopedia illustrates the point: the project developed through adaptation and editing rather than simply reproducing an English reference work in another language. The institutional account should be read as a history of a particular programme, not a description of every publisher or a neutral assessment of cultural influence. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "digital-persian-text",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Persian text has specific digital requirements",
        "content": "Digital production adds questions that are easy to overlook when reading only an English translation. Persian uses an Arabic-derived script written from right to left, while numbers and embedded passages in left-to-right scripts follow their own direction. A Persian article containing an English product name or a numerical expression therefore involves mixed-direction text. [s3]\n\nThe World Wide Web Consortium's Arabic & Persian Layout Requirements addresses support for this writing system in web technologies and electronic publications. Its scope explicitly distinguishes Persian from Standard Arabic and excludes Cyrillic-written Tajik. That distinction is useful for international readers: related languages and a shared script do not make all written conventions interchangeable. The document is a Group Draft Note, so it should be understood as technical requirements work rather than a binding publishing standard. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "authorship-and-rights",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Authorship, attribution, and rights require separate attention",
        "content": "A historical account of Iranian copyright distinguishes rights connected to a creator's authorship from transferable rights of commercial exploitation. It also discusses translation, photographs, and audiovisual works, showing that written production belongs to a wider history of creative rights. [s4]\n\nThat account was originally published in 1993 and later updated. It cannot establish the rules applicable to a contemporary commission, translation agreement, or digital republication. Its value here is explanatory: crediting an author, obtaining permission, and purchasing production services address different questions. Current legal requirements and contractual practice remain outside this page's verified coverage. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
